    "conclusion": "Zero pairs reach CONFIRMED and S4 never fires: GUDID identifies a device by UDI-DI and MDALL by Health Canada licence/device/identifier numbers, so the two registries share no identifier namespace and no keyless official-identifier bridge exists. S1 (exact brand-name equality) never fires in this sample either, and S2 (a shared model token) never fires under either model-token definition — the manufacturer model spaces do not collide across these two sources. What is left is firm attribution plus loose name-token overlap, which yields UNKNOWN for every pair.",
    "no_pair_is_refutable_either": "There are also zero REJECTED pairs. That is not a bug: name-level evidence can no more refute sameness than confirm it. Only an official field can do either, and no official field on these two records contradicts a pair in this sample. A pipeline that emits 'different device' from a name comparison would be over-claiming in the same way as one that emits 'same device'.",
    "sensitivity_note": "Both model-token definitions (strict: letter+digit; lenient: any token containing a digit) produce identical verdict tables, because no model token is shared between the GUDID and MDALL samples at all. The sensitivity pass is retained as a control against a claim that the strict rule was doing the work.",
    "what_would_be_needed": "A shared identifier cannot be synthesised from these two sources. Either a jurisdiction publishes a UDI-DI alongside its national licence numbers (EUDAMED does, for the EU, via Basic UDI-DI), or identity must be established against a third reference that carries both — not by name matching."
